upt and chaos has  erupted so it's up to us to figure out  what happened  the ascent was made by a team of 12  called neon giant studio  googling their company name uncovers  that this is the studio's first game  that doesn't mean the devs are new to  the industry as the four founding  members have worked on games such as  bullet storm wolfenstein doom gears of  war and far cry 3.  now that the rundown is over we can get  to the gameplay and to get the obvious  out of the way like i said the game is  solo and co-op since i got the game  early i didn't find any co-op partners  to play with so all the game will be  from a solo player perspective but you  can play co-op online or  couch co-op if you have a buddy at your  house the ascent's unique mechanic or at  least unique to me was the ability to  take cover and alternate your firing  stance  running around and shooting is a very  effective and ideal way to kill enemies  but you can also crouch behind cover the  issue is that you can't shoot behind  cover because of the game's isometric  camera and combat mechanics  so by pressing the left trigger it aims  higher allowing you to shoot while in  cover or shoot someone at a slightly  higher elevation than you personally the  only game that i've played with this  camera angle that i can recall is diablo  and cover was never needed in a game  like that so adding both these elements  together was quite interesting it does  take some time getting used to but  eventually it will be second nature  going back and forth between cover and  running around when needed  just like any other rpg you have your  health stat a sort of magical ability in  this case called energy  as well as skill points and a variety of  armor guns and skills to choose from  and since this is an rpg game quests  have recommended levels to be at when  starting the quest and some areas are  level gated  going back to the basic shooting it's  actually quite fun  this game does require a bit of aim so  there is definitely a small learning  curve at the start but thankfully every  gun has infinite ammo so there's no need  to worry about it the game gives you the  option  of carrying two weapons of your choosing  as well as one tactical grenade two  augmentations that serve as your  abilities  two modules serving as more of passive  buffs and three  armor pieces your head torso and legs  each time you level up you gain three  skill points and you can put these into  any of the eight available skills some  of which can increase your health  critical hit rate or dodge cooldown you  can put 20 points into each skill  totaling a required 160 points or  53 levels to fully complete all the  skills now i haven't reached the max  rank but i can't imagine it being  anywhere close to level 50 as i feel  like that would ruin the fun of a build  if everyone is basically the same  character with almost maxed out stats  my hypothesis is that it's more closer  to 30 or 35.  like i said i haven't achieved this yet  so i'm not sure but that would seem more  acceptable there was also a bunch of  other nitty-gritty details like damage  types damage resistances as well as  crafting systems that are fairly  self-explanatory so i won't explain them  too much  but the damage types and resistances are  very similar to other games that you may  have played with these  some guns do better against other  enemies and some don't  same goes for the armor some have better  resistance to fire but  some are better at physical damage the  crafting is pretty basic too  since you just need components to rank  up the gun increasing its mark rank  after mark 5 it goes to the next  component and i assume it mark 10 it  will go to the very last rare  component of course to buy sell and  upgrade you need the vendors and at  least one area per level has a hub you  can do everything in from upgrading  selling to cashing in bounties for money  this leads me then to the map  the intention is that you slowly go up  in the floor so you start out at the  bottom floor in the deep stink then go  to the warrens and so on  to get to each level you must take an  elevator but you can then take a train  or eventually a taxi to certain places  on the map which protects that gameplay  loop from being plagued by a constant  walking and backtracking you can't take  a taxi from the warrens to the deep  stinks since they're on different levels  but you can take a taxi to any of the  named locations inside the warrens fast  traveling with the taxi costs about a  thousand credits a trip and while in the  early game it is quite expensive as you  get into the later levels it's quite  easy to acquire a thousand credits  but regardless one of the big reasons  it's so large is because there's tons of  loot to find  simply looking on the map will show all  the available loot chests you can  collect and there's lots to find in  these containing money  components and maybe some gear but this  is where it  loses me the game seems to pride itself  on exploration but not  too much exploration here's an example  on the warren's level which you get  access to around level 3. lots of  enemies are very low ranks as you would  expect  after a couple main missions you can do  a bunch of side missions naturally i did  the level 2 recommended quests first  since it was the easiest except numerous  problems arose because of this  one is that the elevator to get to the  location is locked as you need to  progress in the story to access it which  baffles me why i was given it now  instead of later but not even that also  10 feet away  are literal level 16 enemies who can  easily one shot me to reinforce my point  let's look at an actual main quest  i was told to go to the grinder district  in this quest which is behind this  turret  most players would see this path then  the alternate path to the right and take  that one first as it doesn't lead to the  main quest and there might be some extra  loot you can grab  the only problem is by simply going off  the beaten path for a little over 10  feet the enemies go from level 4 to  level 10.  this happened again on my way to the  grinder district where i arrived at a  town called gratitude on my way to the  main quest  i took a detour into gratitude and was  immediately killed by level 13  enemies it's just surprising how much of  a level jump these enemies have when  they're literally this close to the main  quest  anyone who wants to explore will be hit  with this unfortunate issue and it could  ruin the exploration for them  another issue i have is while i do  understand solo is always going to be  harder  than co-op sometimes i feel this game  really is geared towards co-op  only while cover is a necessity to  living in this game we think  enemies would at least adopt the same  strategy too and you'd be correct except  90 of them seem to not care they will  rush and flank you constantly making  cover practically useless when there's  at least 7 or 8 enemies at once on the  field  it makes it even worse when there's the  more tougher bullet spongy enemies in  the area  ultimately leading to your death either  way in this instance i was supposed to  hold out for 3 minutes and it's not too  terrible until the enemies start coming  out in waves and i'm overwhelmed  i can't run and gun because i'll get  shot and while i can dodge it's only for  so long since there's a cooldown my only  option is to take cover but i can't do  that either since i'm getting surrounded  i don't have an issue with enemies  rushing or flanking me i feel like the  game would be too easy if everyone  stayed behind cover but that's what i  mean when i say the game seems more  co-op oriented at times as even with  just a team of two it would make this  encounter much  easier to handle one small contributor  to this problem is the camera the camera  doesn't track the player merely move  with the player as if it tracked them  they'd be in the center for the 