 the only problem is by simply going off  the beaten path for a little over 10  feet the enemies go from level 4 to  level 10.  this happened again on my way to the  grinder district where i arrived at a  town called gratitude on my way to the  main quest  i took a detour into gratitude and was  immediately killed by level 13  enemies it's just surprising how much of  a level jump these enemies have when  they're literally this close to the main  quest  anyone who wants to explore will be hit  with this unfortunate issue and it could  ruin the exploration for them  another issue i have is while i do  understand solo is always going to be  harder  than co-op sometimes i feel this game  really is geared towards co-op  only while cover is a necessity to  living in this game we think  enemies would at least adopt the same  strategy too and you'd be correct except  90 of them seem to not care they will  rush and flank you constantly making  cover practically useless when there's  at least 7 or 8 enemies at once on the  field  it makes it even worse when there's the  more tougher bullet spongy enemies in  the area  ultimately leading to your death either  way in this instance i was supposed to  hold out for 3 minutes and it's not too  terrible until the enemies start coming  out in waves and i'm overwhelmed  i can't run and gun because i'll get  shot and while i can dodge it's only for  so long since there's a cooldown my only  option is to take cover but i can't do  that either since i'm getting surrounded  i don't have an issue with enemies  rushing or flanking me i feel like the  game would be too easy if everyone  stayed behind cover but that's what i  mean when i say the game seems more  co-op oriented at times as even with  just a team of two it would make this  encounter much  easier to handle one small contributor  to this problem is the camera the camera  doesn't track the player merely move  with the player as if it tracked them  they'd be in the center for the entire  time  while this isn't terrible sometimes it's  atrocious where 70  of my screen is an unplayable area i  can't even get to so it makes no sense  to even show it especially during combat  this makes it easier for enemies to  ambush and surround me without me  knowing since the camera isn't showing  them  even if there were only a few enemies it  would still be nice to have the  character in the middle of my screen so  my aim is more accurate one thing that  is incredibly crucial is  upgrades usually in an rpg game there's  really no point to upgrading your weapon  since there's going to be a new one that  replaces it within the hour  the ascent doesn't really work like that  and it took me a few hours to understand  this  it seems to want you to actually focus  on the damage type and upgrade  here's an example eventually you'll  fight this spider boss and my main  source of damage was bullets  bullets are ineffective against robots  but by simply changing the shotgun i had  to a different energy shotgun and  upgrading it instead of saving the  components the fight was a cakewalk  so while i still think a good portion of  the areas is balanced around co-op  and not solo what really can save you on  occasion is upgrading and damage types  this pairs nicely with the plethora of  loot around the world giving you tons of  opportunities to craft  overall the gameplay is pretty solid it  gives the player lots of options to  create unique characters from skills to  weapons and armor  and the combat dynamic of alternating  from cover to run and gun works for a  majority  of the encounters if you're someone who  has a group of friends or is willing to  go into online co-op with randoms you're  gonna have a fun time  as for a completely solo player it's  possible but it might be tougher than  you think which could turn some players  off  all of this killing and upgrading and  walking all takes place in the arcology  so let's move on to the setting and talk  about the story along with it  simply put it's gorgeous it's crazy how  much detail  is actually in this game there's bags  people and objects everywhere  truly the highlight of this game it was  a clear progression in the environment  starting in the sewer tunnels of the  deep stink moving to the more livable  warrens then even the more extravagant  high street  even though each area per level is  somewhat the same which does have its  drawbacks from a level to level  comparison they are completely different  something i can't get over is the npc's  reaction to the combat  the combat takes place where npcs are  walking so they'll run at the sound of  gunfire but can also get caught up in  the fighting and can be  shot at the fact that whenever i want i  can just kill a group of npcs is quite  the interesting feeling  there isn't some law enforcement that'll  stop you either which kind of fits with  the theme  seeing as everyone at the bottom is  expendable so a few lives lost is no big  deal and it's even further reinforced by  the plethora of npcs that there are even  if all they do they're a stand and don't  talk it still adds to the overall  environment making it feel like what an  arcology would be  a very large but tight tower with  hundreds of thousands of people in one  area all crammed together  it feels alive and that's what a setting  should be for the story i'm going to  refrain from talking about the later  missions as i don't want to spoil the  later content but if you want to jump  into this completely spoiler free  skip to the time on screen to get to the  last section where we talk about  performance and my overall opinion the  story is actually what intrigued me in  the first place  i had to google what an arcology was as  i had never heard about this word before  this game  but after learning that and reading the  brief synopsis at the beginning it was  something that piqued my interest  and arcology is supposed to be a  self-sufficient tower you live  eat sleep and die in here you never need  to leave the building it has all your  basic necessities but also leisure  activities as well  basically take any city from its  population to its buildings and  businesses and cram it into a giant  skyscraper and that's what you get you  see what i mean you never have to leave  the tower that's why this plot is all  the more fascinating what would happen  if the tower you live in 24 7 was  suddenly not under anyone's command and  the leaders originally owning the tower  went bankrupt this affects everyone from  the top to the bottom  speaking to a character we meet later  named poon we discover that even our  paychecks are on standby so there's no  money given out to the workers this can  lead to poverty  and homelessness and when no one has an  answer to the problem riots can start  and seeing as most people are packing  heat in this tower many people could die  in here this alone  is great and the main reason i want to  continue playing during the story you'll  meet a wide variety of creatures all of  whom have their own personalities making  them seem like actual unique characters  not copy pasted versions of each other  sadly in its one criticism i understand  but still feel the need to bring up this  is only for the main campaign  any characters you meet in the main  story are professionally voiced with  unique dialogue separating them from  each other but the side missions don't  have this  all characters dialogue is garbled or  gibberish and either voiced by the same  person with different levels of audio  being raised to make it sound different  or only by a couple people in gt  operative you'll have access to select  gear and you'll be covered by a premium  y corp insurance policy  this is what i meant earlier i want to  criticize this but i feel like this was  due to having a small budget  as paying for professional or even  amateur voice actors is lots of money  especially when making an rpg which can  have hundreds of thousands of characters  that the player can speak to throughout  the game it didn't necessarily ruin my  experience with the game but after a  while it did start to bother me and i  just skipped the side mission dialogue  altogether  of course as stated before the main  missions characters and dialogue was  great but this however was lacking  overall the ascent's story is quite  unique with a premise i haven't seen in  other games that i've played which is  always a good start the main characters  are a joy to listen to even if the  vulgar humor does get annoying sometimes  i just can't say the same for the side  missions  the main quest is quite slow but  definitely does pick up later eventually  reeling you into the narrative  the side missions content is nice too  and gives the player a means of leveling  up with a small amount of story to flesh  out the world a bit  moving on to the last section is the  performance and bugs now this is usually  where most reviewers would list their pc  specs but seeing as i played on a series  x i don't need to do that but i do have  a few changes i would like to at least  be considered by the dev team since most  of these were quite bothersome  in terms of quality i didn't have any  frame issues at all nothing was frozen  or laggy the game ran  very smoothly there was one instance  where i was stuck in some geometry  thankfully the enemies did kill me  allowing me to reset but then i crashed  almost immediately after respawning this  is the one and only time i not only got  stuck in place but also crashed during  my playthrough so i didn't really let  this bother me the rest are changes that  either need to be added or removed  simply for quality of life  one huge issue was the ui text i play on  a series x connected to my tv from about  10 feet away  i checked this on a monitor and it's not  terrible but if your setup is anywhere  close to mine be very mindful of this  for the main hud the bottom right and  bottom left are very hard to read  especially the xp number it's so tiny i  can barely tell without looking at it  for a few seconds what the number is  the tech should be big enough to see  everything at a glance as there's no  time to squint at the screen when in  combat  another is the damage resistance and  damage type numbers on armor and weapons  especially when the number is red  as that coupled with the red background  is very painful to read  some of the tutorials are also culprits  of this too but especially the tactical  grenades i literally couldn't tell you  from where i was sitting what button i  was supposed to press i had to wing it  during combat to figure out how to use  them  speaking of tutorials every time i  logged back onto the game the game gave  me the same tutorials again  you can simply turn them off but i  didn't know when a new mechanic was  going to get implemented so i didn't  want to miss out on key information  it also didn't help that to close the  tutorial i had to hold b  which after the fifth time was pretty  tedious this then moves me to my next  problem  the menu i don't know why but when i go  through the menu there's this noise that  plays when i go from tab to tab  but if i go too quickly it overlaps and  plays a much louder sound  i have no idea why this sound is even  here if the sound was intended to play  either change the noise or just honestly  remove it but arguably the worst  offender and the most ear shattering  noise i had to deal with was when you're  indoors or near an indoor area i  understand the dev's idea behind this  since it's inside the sound has to have  a little bit of an echo as it would in  real life but they're amplified by a ton  and play  way longer than they need to  send you a present  this happened in just about every area  that was either indoors or near a door  that would lead to an indoor area  i personally don't know how to fix this  i would just assume either revert the  audio to match the outside and do away  with the attempted realism or  tune it down a bit besides this i do  have one change i would like added or at  least considered and that's rotating the  map  unless i miss something you can't rotate  the map at all it's incredibly painful  trying to see where i  am in relation to a quest or object but  can't tell since i can't rotate the  camera this would also benefit players  who are stuck  or lost thanks to the large amount of  twists and turns the map has  the ascent overall is a good game for 26  dollars you get the elements of an rpg  with a captivating plot as well as the  option to explore the arcology's  numerous levels to your heart's content  while there is some bugs and mechanics  that do bother me greatly i think the  positives of this game can outweigh the  negatives especially if you have game  pass  as what do you have to lose we have now  reached the end of the review  do let me know what you think about the  ascent as well as the new series down  below  as i'm always looking for feedback thank  you all for stopping by  if you're new make sure to subscribe for  more reviews like this as well as essays  on your favorite games  i highly recommend my recent upload in  which i explain why fallout 76  is one of my favorite games of 2021 as  always if you're a returning viewer i  thank you for supporting me by coming  back for another video  and with that take care everyone and  [Music]  goodbye  [Music]  you
 

